From 5ef2473e41661d8dd3e833338a6ad86cf42b8412 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: scada <scada@noreply.codeberg.org>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 06:32:54 +0400
Subject: [PATCH 79/80] arm64: dts: qcom: msm8998: DDR floors with CPU
 frequency, CPU PMU

The CPU BWMON alone raised DDR too late for bursty work: the big cores
got 6 GB/s copying a 1 MB buffer and 507 ns memory latency where the
vendor kernel, which held DDR at its top step whenever the big cluster
ran at 2457.6 MHz (mincpubw/memlat), gets far more. Vote DDR floors from
the CPU OPP tables over the CPUs' interconnect path: 7.76 GB/s from
1344 MHz and 11.86 GB/s (1555 MHz DDR) from 1958.4 MHz on the big
cluster, 1.5 GB/s at the little cluster's top. Two steps only, so the
frequent schedutil switches rarely change the vote. The 1804 MHz step
is left out: held by the CPUs it wedged the SoC within minutes.
Result: 17 GB/s 1 MB copies and 330 ns latency on the big cores, stable
through a 5 minute all-core memory stress and a 10 minute idle soak.

Also describe the Kryo 280 PMU (PPI 6, as the vendor kernel's cpu-pmu),
so perf and simpleperf have hardware counters.

